 questions: [
  ["A 48-year-old has a 3.5 cm diffuse white reticular and erosive lesion on the right buccal mucosa. The most appropriate biopsy approach is:","Incisional biopsy of a representative area",["Excisional biopsy of the whole area with margins","Brush cytology alone, without any tissue sampling","Fine-needle aspiration through the centre of the lesion"],"Large or diffuse lesions are sampled, not removed, because the aim is diagnosis. Excising a diffuse mucosal condition would create a large defect with no benefit."],
  ["A 39-year-old has a 6 mm firm, pink, sessile nodule on the buccal mucosa along the occlusal line near 3.6. It is unchanged for 2 years. The most appropriate management is:","Excisional biopsy with histological examination",["Incisional biopsy followed by a referral for radiotherapy","Toluidine blue staining followed by annual review","Brush cytology followed by cauterising the nodule"],"A small, clinically benign lesion, such as a traumatic fibroma, is diagnosed and treated in one step by excision. All excised tissue should still go to a pathologist."],
  ["When taking an incisional biopsy of a large ulcerated lesion, the sample should ideally include:","The most suspicious area plus adjacent normal tissue",["The necrotic centre of the ulcer, where tumour is densest","Normal mucosa well away from the ulcer, to avoid seeding","The surface slough scraped from the ulcer base"],"The junction shows the transition from normal to abnormal, and the most suspicious area gives the best chance of finding the worst histology. Necrotic tissue is often non-diagnostic."],
  ["Routine oral biopsy specimens should be placed immediately in:","10% buffered formalin",["Sterile normal saline","Michel's transport medium","Absolute ethanol"],"Formalin preserves tissue architecture for standard histology, with volume well in excess of the specimen. Michel's medium is reserved for direct immunofluorescence."],
  ["A 52-year-old has oral blisters with suspected pemphigus vulgaris. The sample for direct immunofluorescence should be taken from:","Perilesional mucosa, placed fresh or in Michel's medium",["The ulcer base, placed in 10% formalin","The centre of a blister, placed in saline","Normal skin of the forearm, placed in 10% formalin"],"Perilesional tissue retains intact epithelium with bound antibody, whereas ulcerated tissue has lost it. Formalin is a poor medium for immunofluorescence."],
  ["The chief limitation of brush cytology of a suspicious oral lesion is that it:","Samples surface cells and cannot assess invasion",["Requires a general anaesthetic for most patients","Leaves a defect that needs suturing afterwards","Causes dissemination of the tumour into the bloodstream"],"Without full-thickness tissue, the relationship of the cells to the basement membrane cannot be seen, so invasion cannot be assessed. An abnormal result, or a negative one in a suspicious lesion, needs a scalpel biopsy."],
  ["Toluidine blue may stain a benign traumatic ulcer. Which of the following best explains this false positive?","The dye binds nucleic acids, which abound in regenerating tissue",["The dye binds keratin, which thickens at the edge of traumatic ulcers","The dye reacts with mucin released by ruptured salivary ducts","The dye binds haemoglobin in the capillaries of the ulcer base"],"Rapidly dividing, inflamed cells take up the dye much like dysplastic cells, reducing specificity. It can therefore only support, and never replace, histological assessment."],
  ["Local anaesthetic for a soft tissue biopsy should be deposited away from the lesion. Which of the following best explains this?","Injecting into the lesion distorts the tissue architecture",["Injecting into the lesion raises the anaesthetic's systemic toxicity","Injecting nearby gives a longer duration by mixing with mucin","Adrenaline in the lesion turns the specimen permanently blue"],"Fluid within the specimen separates tissue planes and creates artefact for the pathologist. Infiltrating around the lesion gives adequate anaesthesia with haemostasis."],
  ["Aspiration of a central radiolucency in the left mandible of a 25-year-old returns pulsatile bright red blood. The most appropriate action is to:","Stop, apply pressure and refer for vascular assessment",["Continue with an incisional biopsy through a larger flap","Curette the lesion to control the bleeding","Extract the adjacent tooth to decompress the lesion"],"A pulsatile aspirate suggests a vascular lesion such as an arteriovenous malformation, and opening one can cause life-threatening bleeding. Aspiration is done first precisely to avoid this."],
  ["A clinically indurated, speckled lesion in the floor of the mouth of a smoker is reported as hyperkeratosis with no dysplasia. The most appropriate next step is to:","Contact the pathologist and arrange resampling or referral",["Reassure the patient and discharge from follow-up","Review the lesion in 1 year with a new photograph","Prescribe an antifungal and consider the case closed"],"When a benign report does not match the clinical picture, the sample may have missed the critical area. The clinician is responsible for correlating the two, and reassurance is the dangerous error."],
  ["An incisional biopsy of a large leukoplakia on the ventral tongue shows moderate epithelial dysplasia. Which of the following is the most appropriate interpretation?","The lesion is potentially malignant; unsampled areas may be worse",["The lesion has invaded the tissue and needs a neck dissection","The lesion is benign and can be discharged without further follow-up","The sample proves that the whole lesion has the same grade"],"Dysplasia confined to the epithelium is not invasion, but one sample cannot represent the whole lesion, so specialist management and follow-up are needed. Treating the report as benign would be unsafe."],
  ["Which of the following findings most warrants urgent referral for suspected malignancy?","Lower lip numbness with unexplained mobility of 3.6 and 3.7",["A bony hard midline swelling of the hard palate since teenage years","A corticated oval radiolucency below the mandibular canal","Multiple yellow submucosal granules on both buccal mucosae"],"New numbness with unexplained mobility suggests a tumour invading bone and nerve. The other findings describe a torus, a Stafne defect and Fordyce granules, all of which are normal variants."],
  ["A 55-year-old has a 7 mm ulcer on the lateral tongue next to a sharp cusp of 3.6. The ulcer is not indurated. The cusp is smoothed. The most appropriate plan is to:","Review in about 2 weeks and biopsy if it has not healed",["Biopsy immediately, without removing the local cause","Review in about 6 months at the next recall","Prescribe a topical steroid and discharge from follow-up"],"A traumatic ulcer should heal once its cause is removed, so failure to heal within about 2 weeks demands tissue. A 6-month recall is the classic delay that lets carcinoma progress."],
  ["A 60-year-old has a firm mass in the tail of the left parotid gland. The preferred initial tissue sampling technique is:","Ultrasound-guided fine-needle aspiration",["Open incisional biopsy through the overlying skin","Brush cytology of the parotid duct orifice","Punch biopsy taken through the buccal mucosa"],"An open incision through a parotid tumour risks seeding tumour cells and damaging the facial nerve. Fine-needle aspiration gives cellular information with minimal disruption."],
  ["A 62-year-old has a soft blue-purple lesion on the lower lip that blanches under gentle pressure from a glass slide. Which of the following is the most appropriate conclusion?","It is a vascular lesion, and it should not be casually biopsied",["It is a melanoma, and it needs an immediate excisional biopsy","It is an amalgam tattoo, and it needs a radiograph only","It is a mucocele, and it needs excision with the feeding glands"],"Blanching means blood is being pushed out of vessels, as in a venous lake. Melanin and amalgam pigment do not blanch, and a mucocele does not empty on light pressure."]
 ]
